py for Mpox is currently recommended.", "runs": [{"text": "No"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "routine"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "antiviral"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "therapy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Mpox"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "currently"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "recommended."}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "1770"}}, {"id": "body-04155", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Mpox is treated with supportive care for symptoms such as pain and fever, with close attention to nutrition, hydration, skin care, prevention of secondary infections and treatment of co-infections, including HIV if present. Individuals with HIV and Mpox should continue taking their antiretroviral therapy (ART). ART should be initiated within 7 days of diagnosis of HIV.", "runs": [{"text": "Mpox is treated with supportive care for symptoms such as pain and fever, with close attention to nutrition, hydration, skin care,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "prevention"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "secondary"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "infections"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "treatment"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "co-infections,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "including"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "HIV"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "if"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "present."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Individuals"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "HIV"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Mpox should continue taking their antiretroviral therapy (ART). ART should be initiated within 7 days of diagnosis of HIV."}]}, {"id": "body-04156", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Getting an Mpox vaccine can help prevent infection (pre-exposure prophylaxis). It is recommended for people at high-risk of getting Mpox, especially during an outbreak. The vaccine can also be administered after a person has been in contact with someone who has Mpox (post-exposure prophylaxis). In these cases, the vaccine should be given less than 4 days after contact with someone who has Mpox. The vaccine can be given for up to 14 days if the person has not developed symptoms.", "runs": [{"text": "Getting an Mpox vaccine can help prevent infection (pre-exposure prophylaxis). It is recommended for people at high-risk of getting Mpox, especially during an outbreak. The vaccine can also be administered after a person has been in contact with someone who has Mpox (post-exposure prophylaxis). In these cases, the vaccine should be given less than 4 days after contact"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "someone"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "who"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "has"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Mpox."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "The"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "vaccine"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "can"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "given"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "up"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "14"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "days"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "if"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "person"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "has"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "not"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "developed"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "symptoms."}]}, {"id": "body-04158", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Public Health Control Measures", "runs": [{"text": "Public"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Health"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Control"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Measures"}]}, {"id": "body-04159", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Isolate at home (if mild and per healthcare provider guidance) or in hospital (if severe or high-risk transmission concerns) for the full infectious period — from onset of symptoms until all lesions have crusted, scabs have fallen off, and new skin has formed (typically 2–4 weeks).",